<body>
  <GameNav />
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Rooms &amp; Exits</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Chapter 2</a> &gt; Level 17</nav>

  <main class="seo-section">
    <h1>Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 17 Walkthrough: Shoe Store</h1>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Webelinx Games, the developer of Rooms &amp; Exits: Escape Room Games.</div>

    <div class="verify-note">Rebuilt frame by frame from a 7 minute 37 second recorded playthrough of the current build, extracted at one frame per second and read at full resolution, with a second recording used to cross-check the results screen. Everything printed on this page was read off the footage twice from separate frames: the 96 grid, the 31 / 23 / 18 panel, the 2120 till and the five symbols on the note. Two things are deliberately not written as answers, because our capture never settles them: the exact arrow sequence on the golden box, and the order in which the four symbol plates have to be read. Where that happens the page says so rather than guessing.</div>

    <LevelSelector chapter={2} current={17} variant="jump" label="Chapter 2 · No Honor Among Thieves" />

    <div class="answer-box">
      <span class="label">Quick answer</span>
      <p>Three numbers run this level: <b>96</b> for the red arithmetic box, <b>2120</b> for the till, and <b>31 / 23 / 18</b> for the wall panel. Finish worth <b>+5 coins</b>, at <b>6:59</b> on the clock.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Open the white cabinet, work the <b>golden box</b> and take the <b>scissors</b> and the <b>shoes</b>.</li>
        <li>Tap the <b>red box</b> on the counter, read the grid and type <b>96</b> → <b>scroll</b> → dismantle it → <b>piece of paper</b>.</li>
        <li>Clear the <b>shoe-box wall</b> (<b>ARRANGE LOWER TO HIGHER</b>, US 4 to 8.5) → <b>plug</b>.</li>
        <li>Link the wall panel: <b>20 + 11 = 31</b>, <b>14 + 9 = 23</b>, and <b>6 + 7 + 5 = 18</b>.</li>
        <li>Read the four symbols over the shoe display and enter <b>2120</b> on the <b>till</b> → the drawer opens.</li>
        <li>Light the <b>candle</b> with the <b>matches</b>, clear the back room, and combine <b>pencil + piece of paper</b> for the five-symbol row.</li>
        <li>Match the shoes to the soles, take the <b>shoelaces</b>, combine them with the <b>sneaker</b>, put it on the <b>display stand</b> → <b>key card</b> → exit door.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="video-wrap">
            <div class="yt-facade" data-yt="NmmAlRCgssg" data-title="Rooms &amp;amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 17 Shoe Store walkthrough"
           role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Play video: Rooms &amp;amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 17 Shoe Store walkthrough">
        <img src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/NmmAlRCgssg/maxresdefault.jpg"
             alt="Rooms &amp;amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 17 Shoe Store walkthrough" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1280" height="720"
             onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src='https://i.ytimg.com/vi/NmmAlRCgssg/hqdefault.jpg'">
      </div>
    </div>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l17-hero-shoe-store.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 2 Level 17 Shoe Store: isometric view of the boutique with a glass counter on the left, a low table of gift boxes in the middle and a lit shoe display wall on the right" width="1200" height="594" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The Shoe Store is level seventeen of Chapter 2 and it is the biggest room in the chapter so far. It is a real boutique rather than a puzzle box: a glass display counter runs along the left with a golden wooden box sitting inside it, a low table in the middle of the floor holds a pair of pink sandals and a stack of wrapped gift boxes, and the whole right-hand wall is a shoe display lit from underneath with a row of symbol plates mounted above it. Behind the counter there is a till, a SALE poster and a doorway through to a stockroom, and the front of the shop is a glass door with EXIT over it.</p>
    <p>That layout is the reason the level feels long. It is not one chain of three locks in one small room; it is two rooms plus a stockroom, with roughly twenty inventory items passing through your bag and at least four separate puzzles that do not depend on each other. You can solve the arithmetic box without ever looking at the shoe-box wall, and you can clear the number panel without knowing that the till exists. Nothing in the game tells you what order to do any of it in, and the inventory fills up with things whose use only becomes obvious ten minutes later.</p>
    <p>What holds it together is a single idea the level keeps repeating: a number written somewhere in the room, and a lock that wants that number. The four-row grid on the red box is a pattern that has to be continued. The wall panel is three sums that have to be linked. The till is four symbols that have to be counted. The shoe-box wall is ten boxes that have to be ordered. Once you notice that the room is made of number puzzles, the Shoe Store stops being a scavenger hunt and becomes a list of small arithmetic problems with a stockroom attached.</p>
    <p>The recording finishes the room in 6:59 of level time. Players who get stuck here almost always get stuck in one of two places: they never find the note that explains the red box's rule, or they walk past the till because it looks like scenery. Both are covered below, and both are cheap to fix once you know what you are looking at.</p>

    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span></h2>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:04</span>
      <strong>Read the room before you touch anything</strong>
      The first wide shot is the only one you get without an inventory grid over half the screen, so use it. Left wall: the glass counter, the open white cabinet above it, the golden box inside, the till further along. Middle: the low table with the pink sandals and the wrapped gift boxes. Right: the shoe display, lit from under every shelf, with four symbol plates on the wall above it. Back right: the doorway into the stockroom. Front: the glass door you will leave through, which does nothing at all until the very last step. Everything on the left side of the room is number puzzles; everything on the right is the display you read a code off. That split is worth holding on to for the next seven minutes.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NmmAlRCgssg&t=4s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:04</a></div>
    </div>
    <InContentAd />

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:14</span>
      <strong>The low table: pink sandals and a pile of gift boxes</strong>
      The first thing the recording taps is the table in the middle of the floor. The pink braided sandals lift straight into the inventory with the label <b>Sandals</b>, and the wrapped gift boxes around them turn out to be props — you can tap them, they do not open, and nothing in the level ever asks you to come back to them. Take the sandals and move on. The gift boxes are the first of several decorative objects in this room that look interactive and are not; the Shoe Store is generous with those, and the fastest way through it is to stop tapping things that have no highlight.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NmmAlRCgssg&t=14s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:14</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 0:17</span>
      <strong>The white cabinet and the golden box</strong>
      Above the counter there is an open white cabinet, and inside it sits a golden wooden box with a carved lid. This is the first real lock in the level. The box is a flat square with a cross-shaped channel cut into the top of it and a red-and-blue dial in the middle, and the recording spends the better part of half a minute turning it before the lid gives. What falls out of it, along with the <b>Scissors</b> you will need later, is the first of the level's two cutting tools — the Shoe Store runs on scissors, and you will use them on a scroll before the level is out.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l17-wooden-box.jpg" alt="The open white cabinet above the counter with the golden wooden box inside it and a blue arrow pointing left" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NmmAlRCgssg&t=17s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:17</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 0:20</span>
      <strong>The dial on the golden box</strong>
      Zoomed in, the lid is a metal plate with a compass-style dial: a red arm, a blue arm and a white arrow in the middle, sitting in a four-way channel. Turning the dial swings the arms around the cross until the plate releases. Our capture shows the dial being worked for a long stretch and then the box opening, but it never shows a rejected position being held long enough to tell you what the plate is checking — so we have not written a sequence here that we could not read twice.
      What we can tell you is what it is not: there is no clue for this dial anywhere in the room. It is a mechanical puzzle, not a code, and the answer is in the turning rather than in a note. If you are stuck on it, keep rotating until the arms seat in the channel; the box opens on the plate and hands you the scissors.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l17-arrow-lock.jpg" alt="Close-up of the golden box lid: a compass-style dial with a red arm, a blue arm and a white arrow in a cross-shaped channel" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NmmAlRCgssg&t=20s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:20</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 0:46</span>
      <strong>Scissors in the bag, then the first pair of shoes</strong>
      By 0:46 the inventory holds the <b>Scissors</b>, and by 0:51 it holds <b>Shoes</b> as well. Both are ordinary pickups — you tap, the item rises out of the frame with its label underneath, and it goes into the bag. The reason to call them out is that the Shoe Store's inventory is not a tidy list of keys: it fills up with shoes, sandals, a sneaker, shoelaces, a broom, a brush, a dustpan, a hammer, a piggy bank, a candle, a plug, a handle and a pencil, and most of them are ingredients for something later rather than things you use where you find them. Take everything the game offers; there is no inventory limit and no penalty for carrying.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NmmAlRCgssg&t=46s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:46</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 1:02</span>
      <strong>The red box: a four-row grid and the answer 96</strong>
      This is the puzzle most people search for, and it is the one the level is most generous about. On the counter there is a red panel with a grid of numbers and a keypad on the right. Read the grid down the rows:
      <ul>
        <li>1 &nbsp; 4 &nbsp; <b>5</b></li>
        <li>2 &nbsp; 5 &nbsp; <b>12</b></li>
        <li>3 &nbsp; 6 &nbsp; <b>21</b></li>
        <li>8 &nbsp; 11 &nbsp; <b>?</b></li>
      </ul>
      The rule is <b>multiply the first two numbers, then add the first one again</b>: 1×4+1=5, 2×5+2=12, 3×6+3=21. The last row is 8×11+8, which is <b>96</b>. The game prints the whole rule on a scrap of paper that appears beside the box — 1×4+1=5 on the first line, 2×5+2=12 on the second, and 8×11+8=96 in red at the bottom — so you do not have to deduce it, only read it. Type <b>96</b> on the keypad and press the green arrow.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l17-arithmetic-box.jpg" alt="The red arithmetic box with the grid 1 4 5, 2 5 12, 3 6 21, 8 11 and the keypad, with the handwritten rule on a scrap of paper beside it" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NmmAlRCgssg&t=62s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:02</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 7 · 1:11</span>
      <strong>The scroll, and what the scissors are for</strong>
      The box opens on a rolled <b>Scroll</b>. Take it, open the inventory and press <b>Dismantle</b> on it: the scroll unwinds into a flat <b>Piece of paper</b>. This is the first place in the level where the verb wheel matters, and it is the step people skip — a scroll looks like a finished object, so it sits in the bag doing nothing while the player hunts for a clue that is already in their possession. The paper is the thing the pencil will be used on later, and it is also the thing that ends up carrying the five symbols for the locker.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NmmAlRCgssg&t=71s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:11</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 8 · 1:36</span>
      <strong>The shoe-box wall: ARRANGE LOWER TO HIGHER</strong>
      The wall of shoe boxes next to the display is a sorting puzzle. The game prints <b>ARRANGE LOWER TO HIGHER</b> across it, and the ten boxes are labelled with US sizes — 4, 4.5, 5, 5.5, 6, 6.5, 7, 7.5, 8 and 8.5 — with four price stickers between them: <b>$120</b>, <b>$139</b>, <b>$150</b> and <b>$165</b>. Sort by the US size on the label, not by the price, and not by the colour of the box. The prices run in the same direction as the sizes, which makes them look like a second sort key and is exactly why people get this wrong: the instruction is about size, and the sticker is decoration.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l17-shoebox-wall.jpg" alt="The shoe-box wall with ten boxes labelled US 4 to 8.5, price stickers of $120, $139, $150 and $165, and the ARRANGE LOWER TO HIGHER overlay" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NmmAlRCgssg&t=96s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:36</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 9 · 2:02</span>
      <strong>The plug, and the socket by the display</strong>
      Clearing the shoe-box wall is what the recording does just before the <b>Plug</b> turns up at 2:02. The plug goes into the socket on the wall beside the shoe display, and the recording does that at about 3:50 — a long time after picking it up, which is why it spends most of the level sitting in the bag looking useless. If you are following along and cannot find where it goes: it is the socket at the end of the display wall, at the height of the lowest shelf, not the one behind the till.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NmmAlRCgssg&t=122s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:02</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 10 · 3:01</span>
      <strong>The number panel: three totals and seven numbers</strong>
      On the wall by the shoe display there is a grey panel with three totals along the top — <b>31</b>, <b>23</b> and <b>18</b> — and a column of numbers down the left: <b>6, 7, 14, 11, 5, 20, 9</b>. The note pinned beside it carries the sums in handwriting: <b>20 + 11 = 31</b> and <b>14 + 9 = 23</b>. You solve it by dragging a line from each number in the left column to the total it belongs to.
      The third total is the one that catches people out, because the note's last line is written in a hand that reads like 6+7+9 — and 9 is already spent on 23. The panel is what settles it: seven numbers, two totals taking two each, leaves exactly three for the last one, and those three are <b>6, 7 and 5</b>. Drag <b>6</b>, <b>7</b> and <b>5</b> to <b>18</b> and the panel lights up.
