    <h2>Every lock in the Shoe Store<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Seven locks, one page. If you only want the numbers, this table is the whole level:</p>
    <table class="code-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Lock</th><th>Where it is</th><th>Answer</th><th>Where the clue comes from</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Golden box dial</td><td>White cabinet above the counter</td><td><b>not published</b> — mechanical</td><td>No clue in the room; rotate until the plate seats</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Red arithmetic box</td><td>Counter, right of the cabinet</td><td><b>96</b></td><td>Handwritten note beside the box: 1×4+1=5, 2×5+2=12, 8×11+8=96</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shoe-box wall</td><td>Left of the shoe display</td><td><b>US 4 → 8.5</b></td><td>Overlay text: ARRANGE LOWER TO HIGHER</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Number panel</td><td>Wall, next to the display</td><td><b>20+11=31, 14+9=23, 6+7+5=18</b></td><td>Note pinned beside the panel</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Till</td><td>Counter, past the red box</td><td><b>2120</b></td><td>Four symbol plates beside the shoe display</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Red locker</td><td>Stockroom</td><td><b>star, club, triangle, crescent, diamond</b></td><td>Pencil combined with the piece of paper</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Display stand</td><td>Shop floor, centre</td><td><b>sneaker + shoelaces</b></td><td>Shoelaces from the sole board, sneaker from the stand</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The table is also the answer to the question everyone asks about this level: how many codes are there? Two pure number codes — <b>96</b> and <b>2120</b> — plus one arithmetic panel that is a code in all but name, one sort, one mechanical dial and one symbol sequence. The Shoe Store is not a level with a single big password; it is a level with a shop's worth of small locks on it.</p>

    <h2>Why 96 is the answer, and not 92 or 88<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>It is worth spelling out, because the arithmetic box is the one puzzle in this level where a wrong answer looks reasonable. There are three worked examples on the grid and one that is not worked:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>1 4 5.</b> 1 × 4 = 4, plus 1 = <b>5</b>.</li>
      <li><b>2 5 12.</b> 2 × 5 = 10, plus 2 = <b>12</b>.</li>
      <li><b>3 6 21.</b> 3 × 6 = 18, plus 3 = <b>21</b>.</li>
      <li><b>8 11 ?.</b> 8 × 11 = 88, plus 8 = <b>96</b>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The rule has to hold for all three examples or it is not the rule, and it does: in every row the third column is the first two multiplied, with the first column added back on top. That is also why 8 × 11 alone — 88 — is the common wrong answer: the third term is visible on the grid and invisible in a hurry. If you typed 88 and nothing happened, you stopped one step early.</p>

    <h2>The order that works<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The Shoe Store does not enforce an order, but it has a natural one, and following it means never carrying an item you cannot use yet:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Front of shop, left to right.</b> Take the sandals from the table, open the cabinet, work the dial and take the scissors. Then clear the red box with 96 and dismantle the scroll into the piece of paper.</li>
      <li><b>The shoe-box wall.</b> Sort it while you are still near the counter; that is where the plug comes from.</li>
      <li><b>The panel.</b> Link 20+11 to 31, 14+9 to 23 and 6+7+5 to 18.</li>
      <li><b>The display wall and the till.</b> Read the four plates, then key 2120 into the till. Plug in the socket on the same wall.</li>
      <li><b>The stockroom.</b> Light the candle, take the cleaning kit, the coin, the handle and the pencil. Combine the pencil with the paper, read five symbols, open the red locker.</li>
      <li><b>Back to the shop floor.</b> Clear the shoe-to-sole board for the shoelaces, combine them with the sneaker, place the sneaker on the stand, take the key card, open the door.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Two of those steps can be swapped without consequence — the shoe-box wall and the number panel do not depend on each other, and the till does not care whether you have cleared the panel. The one hard dependency is at the end: the shoelaces come from the sole board, the sneaker comes from the stand, and the key card comes from putting the two together. If you are missing the shoelaces, you have not finished the board.</p>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The note beside the red box.</b> It appears when you zoom in and it is easy to miss because it sits low and to the left. Without it the grid is just four rows of numbers. With it the box is a reading exercise.</li>
      <li><b>The scroll.</b> It is a scroll until you press <b>Dismantle</b>. Players who never open the inventory on it end up with no piece of paper, and no piece of paper means no pencil clue, and no pencil clue means the locker stays shut.</li>
      <li><b>The till.</b> It looks like a prop. It is the second number lock in the level and the only place 2120 goes. If you have read four symbols and cannot think where to use them, the answer is the machine on the counter, not the panel on the wall.</li>
      <li><b>The four plates.</b> They are small and they sit in the dark beside the display, and the recording has to step in close to read them. If the till is blank, look at the wall beside the shoes rather than at the shoes — but take the digit order off the till screen itself, not off the wall.</li>
      <li><b>The shoe-box wall.</b> The price stickers make it look like a two-key sort. It is not. ARRANGE LOWER TO HIGHER, and the lower number is the US size.</li>
      <li><b>The third total on the panel.</b> 6+7+5, not 6+7+9. The note's handwriting makes the last digit look like a 9 and the panel will not accept it twice, because 9 is already on 23.</li>
      <li><b>The exit.</b> The glass door needs the key card <b>in hand and used</b>. Tapping the door with the card still in the bag does nothing, exactly like every other door in this game.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="warn">
      <b>What we could not verify:</b> the arrow dial on the golden box. The recording turns it for the better part of a minute and the lid releases, but no rejected position is ever held long enough for us to read what the plate is checking, and there is no note anywhere in the room that refers to it. We have described how to work it rather than printed a sequence we could not read twice. The same caution applies to the four symbol plates beside the display: our two reads of that wall disagree on the order they hang in, so we publish the symbols and the till code (2120) and leave the mapping between them out of the guide. Treat 2120 as the code that worked in this recording, not as a rule we could prove.
    </div>

    <h2>Chapter 2 levels<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p class="grid-note">Chapter 2 runs twenty-six rooms under the name No Honor Among Thieves. The grid below shows what is written up so far.</p>
    <LevelSelector chapter={2} current={17} compact={true} label="Chapter 2 · No Honor Among Thieves" />

    <h2>FAQ<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <details><summary>What is the arithmetic box answer in Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 17?</summary><p><b>96</b>. The grid is 1 4 5 / 2 5 12 / 3 6 21 / 8 11 with the last cell empty, and the rule is multiply the first two numbers and add the first one again: 1×4+1=5, 2×5+2=12, 3×6+3=21, so 8×11+8=<b>96</b>. The game prints the rule on a note beside the box. Type 96 and press the green arrow.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How do you solve the 31 / 23 / 18 number panel?</summary><p>Drag a line from each number in the left column to the total it belongs to. <b>20 + 11 = 31</b> and <b>14 + 9 = 23</b> are written on the note pinned beside the panel; the last total, <b>18</b>, takes the three numbers that are left over — <b>6, 7 and 5</b>. Seven numbers for three totals means the third answer is forced once the first two pairs are taken.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What is the till code in the Shoe Store?</summary><p><b>2120</b>. The till shows four symbols across the top — a star, a circle, a flower and a bow — with a four-box digit field under them, and those are the same four symbols as the framed plates beside the shoe display. Enter 2, 1, 2, 0 and the cash drawer springs open. Our two reads of the plate wall disagree on the order the plates hang in, so this page gives you the four digits rather than a symbol-to-digit mapping we could not confirm twice.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What order do the shoe boxes go in?</summary><p>Lowest size to highest. The game prints <b>ARRANGE LOWER TO HIGHER</b> across the wall, and the ten boxes are labelled US 4, 4.5, 5, 5.5, 6, 6.5, 7, 7.5, 8 and 8.5, with four price stickers between them: $120, $139, $150 and $165. Sort by the US size, not by the price.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What are the symbols on the shoe-store note?</summary><p>Five, left to right: a <b>star</b>, a <b>club</b>, a <b>triangle</b>, a <b>crescent moon</b> and a <b>diamond</b>. You only see them after you combine the <b>pencil</b> with the <b>piece of paper</b> in the inventory. That row is the code for the five-symbol lock on the red locker in the stockroom.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Where do I get the key card to leave the Shoe Store?</summary><p>From the display stand after you place the sneaker on it. Take the <b>sneaker</b> off the pedestal, combine it with the <b>shoelaces</b> from the shoe-to-sole board, then use the finished sneaker on the stand. It releases a <b>key card</b>; use that on the glass exit door.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How long does Chapter 2 Level 17 take?</summary><p>The recording finishes the Shoe Store in <b>6:59</b> of level time for a <b>+5</b> coin reward. Nothing is timed and there is no fail state, so the only thing that really costs you time is entering the till code before you have read the four symbols off the display wall.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Do I need to finish everything in the shop to leave?</summary><p>No. Once the key card is in your hand the glass door opens, and the sandals, the shoes, the broom, the brush, the dustpan, the hammer and the piggy bank are all optional. They feed other puzzles inside the level, not the exit.</p></details>

